NAME
       audioinfo - report the sound hardware the kernel found

SYNOPSIS
       audioinfo [-d DEVICE|N] [-a] [-v] [-s] [-p] [-e] [-n]

DESCRIPTION
       audioinfo asks the kernel's sound driver directly  what  it  found  and
       what it can do: the card, its driver and codec and the bus address; the
       sample formats, rates and channel counts  it  plays  and  records;  the
       period and buffer sizes it  accepts;  every  volume  control  with  its
       setting in percent and  in  decibels,  its  range  and  the  number  of
       hardware steps; the mute switches; the outputs and inputs,  with  their
       kind, their jack state and, for HDMI  and  DisplayPort,  the  monitor's
       name; and the driver's own summary of the controller and its streams.

       It uses the card's control node (/dev/mixer for card 0, /dev/mixerN for
       card N), because opening that claims nothing: the audio service,  which
       holds the playback device, keeps playing  while  audioinfo  runs.  Only
       --probe opens the playback node itself.

       The sound devices are owned by root and the group audio with mode 0660,
       so audioinfo works for root and for members of that group.  For  anyone
       else it explains why it cannot look and what to  do,  and  then  prints
       what the audio service reports instead -- the card and  codec  in  use,
       the mixer format, the master volume, the outputs and the cards -- which
       needs no privilege. Ordinary programs never need the device; they  play
       through the service.

       Without -d, card 0 is shown.

OPTIONS
       -d, --device DEVICE
              The card to examine: a card number, or any of  its  nodes,  e.g.
              /dev/audio1, /dev/dsp1 or /dev/mixer1. A bare argument means the
              same.

       -a, --all
              Every card, one after the other.

       -v, --verbose
              Also the driver's full codec dump. For a High  Definition  Audio
              card this lists every codec on the  link  with  its  vendor  and
              revision, every widget of its  function  groups  --  converters,
              mixers, selectors, pin complexes, power and volume knobs -- with
              capabilities, amplifier ranges, connection lists and  the  pins'
              default configuration  (location,  device,  connector,  colour),
              which is what the driver's automatic path finding works from and
              what a missing sound usually comes down to.

       -s, --streams
              Also the driver's stream dump: each  hardware  stream  with  its
              state, format, buffer and position.

       -p, --probe
              Open the playback node and ask which parameters it  grants:  its
              defaults, then each standard rate, each format and each  channel
              count. Nothing is played and the parameters are put  back.  This
              only works while no one else holds the device  --  normally  the
              audio service does, and audioinfo  says  so;  stop  the  service
              first.

       -e, --events
              After the report, print the card's events as they  happen  until
              Ctrl-C: jacks plugged  and  unplugged,  playback  underruns  and
              capture overruns, volume changes, monitors with audio coming and
              going, and the card disappearing. Plug the headphones in and out
              to see whether the jack is sensed.

       -n, --no-summary
              Leave out the driver's summary.

       -h, --help
              A summary of the options.

OUTPUT
       Volumes are shown as 0..100 and,  where  the  hardware  has  a  decibel
       scale, as the gain at the current setting and the control's range, e.g.
       -12.75 dB, range -65.25 dB .. 0.00 dB,  87  steps.  A  control  without
       hardware steps is  applied  in  software.  An  output  marked  with  an
       asterisk is active; "built in" outputs are always present, and "no jack
       sense" means the hardware cannot tell whether something is plugged in.

EXIT STATUS
       0      The  card  was  examined  (or,  without  access,   the   service
              answered).

       1      There is no such card --  the  kernel  has  no  driver  for  the
              machine's sound hardware, or there is none  --  or  neither  the
              device nor the service could be asked.

       2      A bad argument.

FILES
       /dev/audioN
              The native PCM node of card N (crw-rw---- root audio).

       /dev/dsp, /dev/dspN
              The OSS-compatible PCM node of the same card.

       /dev/mixer, /dev/mixerN
              The control node, used for everything except --probe.

SEE ALSO
       audioctl(1), audiotest(1), audiod(1), adduser(8), dmesg(1)

